WEBVTT

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.740
The best agentic engineers aren't the ones

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:08.260
who use agents the most.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:10.040
They're the ones who know exactly when

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:11.300
to put the agent away.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:12.780
Reach for an agent when the job

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:13.680
calls for a human,

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.910
and you lose both time and credibility.

00:00:16.876 --> 00:00:19.136
Every agent interaction has a cost.

00:00:19.136 --> 00:00:20.336
You write the prompt,

00:00:20.336 --> 00:00:20.976
wait,

00:00:20.976 --> 00:00:21.576
review,

00:00:21.576 --> 00:00:22.116
fix,

00:00:22.116 --> 00:00:23.156
and run it again.

00:00:23.156 --> 00:00:24.856
For an hour -long task,

00:00:24.856 --> 00:00:25.786
that's a bargain.

00:00:25.796 --> 00:00:27.226
For renaming a variable,

00:00:27.296 --> 00:00:28.836
it's slower than just doing it.

00:00:28.836 --> 00:00:30.506
In one randomized trial,

00:00:30.576 --> 00:00:32.936
experienced developers in code they knew well

00:00:32.936 --> 00:00:34.936
were slower with AI tools,

00:00:34.936 --> 00:00:37.066
while believing they'd been faster.

00:00:37.316 --> 00:00:39.296
How fast you felt is not a

00:00:39.296 --> 00:00:40.146
measurement.

00:00:40.606 --> 00:00:42.636
When you're designing a system from scratch,

00:00:42.686 --> 00:00:43.946
the thinking is the work.

00:00:43.946 --> 00:00:45.746
An agent can list the trade -offs,

00:00:45.746 --> 00:00:47.486
but if it makes the calls,

00:00:47.486 --> 00:00:49.246
you won't understand your own system.

00:00:49.246 --> 00:00:51.226
That judgment only comes from doing the

00:00:51.226 --> 00:00:51.786
work.

00:00:51.786 --> 00:00:53.676
Use it as a sparring partner,

00:00:53.686 --> 00:00:54.896
not as the architect.

00:00:55.814 --> 00:00:57.694
Security critical code is different.

00:00:57.694 --> 00:00:59.834
A broken auth check shows no symptoms

00:00:59.834 --> 00:01:01.554
until an attacker finds it,

00:01:01.554 --> 00:01:03.714
and agents write code that looks right.

00:01:03.714 --> 00:01:05.634
So write it yourself and get human

00:01:05.634 --> 00:01:06.524
eyes on it.

00:01:06.594 --> 00:01:08.354
Then flip the roles and ask the

00:01:08.354 --> 00:01:10.164
agent to attack your token check.

00:01:10.194 --> 00:01:11.554
Let the agent hunt.

00:01:11.554 --> 00:01:12.584
Keep the pen.

00:01:13.365 --> 00:01:14.285
When you're learning,

00:01:14.285 --> 00:01:15.205
resist it.

00:01:15.205 --> 00:01:17.265
If the agent writes all the Rust,

00:01:17.265 --> 00:01:18.675
you haven't learned Rust.

00:01:19.005 --> 00:01:21.345
Asking why a borrow checker error happens

00:01:21.345 --> 00:01:22.655
builds understanding.

00:01:22.825 --> 00:01:24.915
Asking the agent to fix it doesn't.

00:01:24.945 --> 00:01:26.515
Use agents as tutors,

00:01:26.565 --> 00:01:27.715
not ghostwriters.

00:01:27.885 --> 00:01:29.145
And hard news,

00:01:29.145 --> 00:01:30.755
like sunsetting a product,

00:01:30.805 --> 00:01:32.075
should come from a person.

00:01:32.803 --> 00:01:34.883
Agents amplify what's already there.

00:01:34.883 --> 00:01:36.383
In a clean codebase,

00:01:36.383 --> 00:01:37.643
they write clean code.

00:01:37.643 --> 00:01:38.633
In a mess,

00:01:38.723 --> 00:01:39.743
they write more mess.

00:01:39.743 --> 00:01:41.963
In legacy code you can't clean up,

00:01:41.963 --> 00:01:43.963
start with characterisation tests,

00:01:43.963 --> 00:01:45.193
scope ruthlessly,

00:01:45.283 --> 00:01:46.823
and hand the agent the tedious,

00:01:46.823 --> 00:01:48.093
mechanical parts.

00:01:48.893 --> 00:01:50.113
Then there's craft.

00:01:50.113 --> 00:01:52.513
Writing code by hand builds pattern recognition

00:01:52.513 --> 00:01:53.923
that lives in your fingers.

00:01:54.073 --> 00:01:55.613
When you hit a problem the agent

00:01:55.613 --> 00:01:56.433
can't solve,

00:01:56.433 --> 00:01:57.933
you need those skills sharp.

00:01:57.933 --> 00:01:59.883
So write code by hand regularly.

00:01:59.973 --> 00:02:01.293
Not because it's faster,

00:02:01.293 --> 00:02:02.823
but because it keeps you dangerous.

00:02:03.747 --> 00:02:05.447
The central skill isn't prompting.

00:02:05.447 --> 00:02:06.357
It's judgment.

00:02:06.527 --> 00:02:08.427
Reach for agents when the leverage is

00:02:08.427 --> 00:02:09.207
real:

00:02:09.207 --> 00:02:10.327
big refactors,

00:02:10.327 --> 00:02:11.397
boilerplate,

00:02:11.427 --> 00:02:12.557
exploring code,

00:02:12.667 --> 00:02:13.667
writing tests.

00:02:13.667 --> 00:02:15.287
Put them away when the work needs

00:02:15.287 --> 00:02:16.157
human thinking,

00:02:16.267 --> 00:02:17.217
human accountability,

00:02:17.447 --> 00:02:18.557
or human craft.

00:02:18.707 --> 00:02:20.547
Anyone can type a prompt.

00:02:20.547 --> 00:02:22.567
Knowing when not to is the harder

00:02:22.567 --> 00:02:23.387
skill.

00:02:23.387 --> 00:02:24.147
Next up:

00:02:24.147 --> 00:02:25.257
agentic teams.
